This chapter describes how the library encodes unbounded operators on a complex Hilbert space and
proves the spectral theorem for unbounded self-adjoint operators. It corresponds to
[Section 10.3](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#sctn:unbounded-spectral-theorems)
of the blueprint, which, like Section 10.2, follows Hall's
[_Quantum Theory for Mathematicians_](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4614-7116-5). It builds on the
{ref "spectral"}[bounded spectral theory chapter]: projection-valued measures, the bounded
operator-valued integral and bounded quadratic forms are taken from there. Stone's theorem, in
Section 10.4 of the blueprint, builds on this one.

# Overview
%%%
tag := "unbounded-overview"
%%%

As you will recall, the position operator $`(X\psi)(x) = x\,\psi(x)` and the momentum operator
$`P = -i\,d/dx` on $`L^2(\mathbb{R})` are not bounded, and cannot be defined on every
square-integrable wave function: $`x\,\psi(x)` need not be square-integrable, and $`\psi` need not be
differentiable. By the Hellinger–Toeplitz theorem, a symmetric operator defined on all of a Hilbert
space is bounded, so any observable with unbounded spectrum, such as most Hamiltonians, has to live on a
dense subspace, its _domain_. The domain is part of the operator: $`-i\,d/dx` on smooth compactly
supported functions and on the Sobolev space $`H^1(\mathbb{R})` are different operators, and only the
second is self-adjoint. The spectral theorem holds for self-adjoint operators, and the physicist's
"Hermitian" (symmetric) is not enough.

The spectral theorem for an unbounded self-adjoint operator $`A` says what the bounded one does,
with the domain made explicit: there is exactly one projection-valued measure $`\mu^A` on the Borel
sets of $`\mathbb{R}` with

$$`A = \int_{\mathbb{R}} \lambda \, d\mu^A(\lambda), \qquad
\mathrm{Dom}(A) = \Big\{\psi : \int_{\mathbb{R}} \lambda^2 \, d\mu^A_\psi(\lambda) < \infty\Big\},`

where $`\mu^A_\psi(E) = \langle \psi, \mu^A(E)\psi \rangle`. For a measurable $`f`, $`f(A)` is
$`\int f \, d\mu^A`, defined on the $`\psi` with $`\int |f|^2 \, d\mu^A_\psi < \infty`; the time
evolution $`e^{-it\hat H}` generated by a Hamiltonian $`\hat H` is a bounded case of this.

The section is formalised in five stages, as in the blueprint:

* _Unbounded operators._ An operator is a linear map on a subspace, its domain. The section treats
  dense domains, adjoints, symmetric, self-adjoint and essentially self-adjoint operators, closed and
  closable operators and closures, kernels and ranges, and the resolvent set and spectrum. It proves
  that $`\mathrm{Range}(T)^\perp = \mathrm{Ker}(T^*)`, that the spectrum of a self-adjoint operator
  is real, that an essentially self-adjoint operator has exactly one self-adjoint extension, and the
  criterion: a densely defined symmetric $`T` is essentially self-adjoint exactly when
  $`T - i\mathbf{1}` and $`T + i\mathbf{1}` have dense ranges. An operator that acts by bounded
  self-adjoint operators on the summands of a direct sum is essentially self-adjoint.
* _Integration against a projection-valued measure._ For a measurable, possibly unbounded,
  $`f` and a projection-valued measure $`\mu`, $`\int f \, d\mu` is an unbounded operator with domain
  $`W_f = \{\psi : \int |f|^2 \, d\mu_\psi < \infty\}`. The domain is dense, the operator is
  characterised by $`\langle \psi, (\int f \, d\mu)\psi \rangle = \int f \, d\mu_\psi`, it agrees
  with the bounded integral for bounded $`f`, and it is self-adjoint for real $`f`.
* _Bounded normal operators._ For a bounded normal $`A` the section proves the two-variable
  spectral mapping theorem $`\sigma(p(A, A^*)) = \{p(\lambda, \bar\lambda) : \lambda \in \sigma(A)\}`
  for polynomials $`p`, and the continuous functional calculus on $`C(\sigma(A), \mathbb{C})`,
  characterised as the unique bounded linear map extending $`p \mapsto p(A, A^*)`.
* _The abstract functional calculus._ Any isometric \*-homomorphism
  $`\Phi : C(X, \mathbb{C}) \to \mathcal{B}(H)` on a compact metric space $`X`, sending $`1` to
  $`\mathbf{1}`, comes from a projection-valued measure on $`X`. Applied to the calculus of a
  normal operator, this gives the spectral theorem for bounded normal operators.
* _The Cayley transform._ A self-adjoint $`A` has a unitary Cayley transform
  $`U = (A + i\mathbf{1})(A - i\mathbf{1})^{-1}`. The spectral measure of $`U`, carried from the unit
  circle to $`\mathbb{R}` by the inverse of the Cayley map $`x \mapsto (x + i)/(x - i)`, integrates
  the identity function to $`A`, and it is the only projection-valued measure on $`\mathbb{R}` that
  does. This is the spectral theorem for unbounded self-adjoint operators; the functional calculus
  $`f(A)` follows.

Every statement of the section is proved in Lean, with no `sorry`. The standard results the
blueprint states without proof, such as the monotone and dominated convergence theorems and the
basic facts about $`L^2` and $`\ell^2`, are Mathlib theorems. The spectral theorem is proved for
unbounded self-adjoint and for bounded normal operators; the spectral theorem for unbounded normal
operators is not stated in the blueprint or proved in the library. The declarations are in the
namespace `Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded`, written without the prefix below.

# Design
%%%
tag := "unbounded-design"
%%%

The conventions inherited from Mathlib, among them the adjoint of a partial map and the guarded
integral, are collected in the conventions chapter under
{ref "conventions-implementation"}[Implementation choices].

Where the library departs from the blueprint's route, this section says so, and links the
blueprint's formalization note where it has one. The conventions of the
{ref "spectral-design"}[bounded chapter] carry over unchanged: bounded operators are
`H →L[ℂ] H`, no result on the way to the spectral theorem assumes $`H` separable (only
{apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.completeSpace_and_separableSpace_of_isClosed}[`completeSpace_and_separableSpace_of_isClosed`],
the blueprint's lemma on closed subspaces, does), and bounded operators are obtained from bounded
quadratic forms.

## Unbounded operators are Mathlib's partial linear maps

An unbounded operator is an element of `H →ₗ.[ℂ] H`, Mathlib's `LinearPMap`: a submodule
`T.domain` of $`H` together with a linear map from it to $`H`; `T` is applied to elements of the
subtype `T.domain`. Two partial maps are equal only if their domains are, so every equation
between unbounded operators in the library, such as $`T^* = T`, includes the equality of
domains. Mathlib supplies the order `S ≤ T` ("$`T` extends $`S`"), the sum `S + T` on the
intersection of the domains, the graph, closedness (`LinearPMap.IsClosed`), closability and the
closure (`LinearPMap.IsClosable`, `LinearPMap.closure`). The graph lies in the plain product
`H × H`: every use of it in the section is topological, so the inner product of the blueprint's
product Hilbert space is not needed (the remark on the Lean formalization in
[def:product-hilbert-space](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#def:product-hilbert-space)).
A bounded operator `B` becomes a partial map with domain all of $`H` as
`(B : H →ₗ[ℂ] H).toPMap ⊤`.

Density of the domain is not part of the type. It is the predicate `HasDenseDomain T`, carried as a
hypothesis wherever the blueprint needs it. Symmetry (`IsSymmetric`) assumes no density, so it
applies to extensions not yet known to be densely defined (the blueprint records this in the proof
of
[lmm:self-adjoint-maximally-symmetric](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#lmm:self-adjoint-maximally-symmetric)).

## The adjoint is Mathlib's, with its convention

The adjoint is Mathlib's `LinearPMap.adjoint`, written `T†`. Its domain is
`LinearPMap.adjointDomain`, the vectors $`\varphi` for which $`\psi \mapsto \langle \varphi, T\psi \rangle`
is continuous on the domain of $`T`, which is the blueprint's boundedness condition. Mathlib defines
`T†` for every `T`: when the domain of `T` is not dense, `T†` keeps this domain but is the zero map
on it. The blueprint's adjoint exists only for densely defined operators, so every theorem about
the action of `T†` assumes `HasDenseDomain T`.

Self-adjointness is Mathlib's `IsSelfAdjoint` for the star operation $`T \mapsto T^*` on
`H →ₗ.[ℂ] H`, so `IsSelfAdjoint T` means `T† = T`, domains included. It implies a dense domain
(`IsSelfAdjoint.dense_domain`), which is why theorems about self-adjoint operators, such as the
spectral theorem, carry no separate density hypothesis.

## The resolvent is a predicate

For a bounded operator, $`\lambda` is in the resolvent set when $`A - \lambda\mathbf{1}` is a unit
of the algebra `H →L[ℂ] H`. Partial maps form no ring, so the library spells out the bounded
inverse of $`T - \lambda\mathbf{1}` as the predicate `IsResolvent T lam B` on a bounded `B`. The resolvent set and the
spectrum are `pmapResolventSet T` and `pmapSpectrum T`; the prefix keeps them apart from Mathlib's
`resolventSet` and `spectrum`, with which they agree on bounded operators
(`pmapSpectrum_toPMap_top`). The operator $`T - \lambda\mathbf{1}` is `subSmul T lam` (`λ` is a
Lean keyword). The sign is the blueprint's: `B` is $`(T - \lambda\mathbf{1})^{-1}`, the negative of
Mathlib's `resolvent`.

## The integral of an unbounded function

For a measurable $`f` that may be unbounded, the library follows the blueprint
([prpstn:hall-10.2](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#prpstn:hall-10.2)):

1. The domain $`W_f` is `integralDomain μ f`, the $`\psi` with `MemLp f 2 (μ.assoc ψ)`, Mathlib's
   "square-integrable for $`\mu_\psi`".
2. On $`W_f`, $`Q_f(\psi) = \int f \, d\mu_\psi` is a quadratic form on a subspace
   (`integralForm`, `IsQuadraticFormOn`).
3. $`(\int f \, d\mu)\psi` is the unique $`\chi` with $`L_f(\varphi, \psi) = \langle \varphi, \chi \rangle`
   for all $`\varphi` in the dense subspace $`W_f`, where $`L_f` is the polarization of $`Q_f`.

The action, `integralApply μ f`, is chosen from this existence-and-uniqueness statement and is
guarded: it is $`0` when $`f` is not measurable or no unique $`\chi` exists. The guard makes
`pmapIntegral μ f` a partial linear map for every $`f`, linear without a hypothesis, and so every
theorem about its values assumes `Measurable f`, as theorems about the bounded integral assume
`f ∈ BddMeasurable X`.

The blueprint proves that $`\int f \, d\mu` is self-adjoint for real $`f`
([prpstn:hall-10.3](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#prpstn:hall-10.3))
by decomposing $`H` into the spectral subspaces where $`n - 1 \le |f| < n`, on which the operator is
bounded, and applying its result on direct sums. The Lean proof avoids direct sums:
$`\int f \, d\mu` is symmetric, and for non-real $`c` the bounded integral of $`(f - c)^{-1}` is
a right inverse of $`\int f \, d\mu - c\mathbf{1}`, so the latter maps onto $`H`; a symmetric
operator with this property is self-adjoint (formalization note of prpstn:hall-10.3). The
direct-sum results, {apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.isEssentiallySelfAdjoint_of_isDirectSumOperator}[`isEssentiallySelfAdjoint_of_isDirectSumOperator`] and its internal form, are formalised in their
own right; no proof outside their file uses them. The external direct sum is Mathlib's `lp G 2`.

## Normal operators and the abstract calculus

The Cayley transform of a self-adjoint operator is unitary, hence normal, but in general not self-adjoint, so the
route to the unbounded theorem needs the spectral theorem for bounded normal operators. Normality
is Mathlib's `IsStarNormal`, and the continuous functional calculus `normalCalculus` is Mathlib's
`cfcHom`, as `realCalculus` is in the bounded case; the library adds the blueprint's
characterisation by polynomials in $`A` and $`A^*`. It proves the two-variable spectral mapping
theorem from Mathlib's spectral mapping theorem for the continuous functional calculus, not by
the blueprint's almost-eigenvector argument (formalization note of
[thrm:hall-10.23](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#thrm:hall-10.23));
the almost-eigenvector lemmas are formalised separately.

The passage from a continuous calculus to a projection-valued measure is done once, abstractly, as
in the blueprint. `IsAbstractCalculus Φ` lists the five properties of a map
$`\Phi : C(X, \mathbb{C}) \to \mathcal{B}(H)` on a compact metric space $`X`. The measures
$`\mu_\psi` come from Mathlib's Riesz–Markov–Kakutani theorem, the extended calculus
$`\tilde\Phi(f)` for bounded measurable $`f` from bounded quadratic forms, and the
projection-valued measure `abstractPVM` sends $`E` to $`\tilde\Phi(1_E)`. The spectral theorem for
a bounded normal operator is `abstractPVM` of `normalCalculus`. The bounded self-adjoint theorem of
Section 10.2 is proved there by its own route and is not rederived from `abstractPVM`.

## Why the Cayley transform

The library proves the unbounded spectral theorem as the blueprint does, through the Cayley
transform, which turns the unbounded problem into a bounded one. For self-adjoint $`A`,
{apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.exists_isCayleyTransform}[`exists_isCayleyTransform`] gives a unitary $`U` with $`U - \mathbf{1}` injective with range
$`\mathrm{Dom}(A)`, and $`A = i(U + \mathbf{1})(U - \mathbf{1})^{-1}` on $`\mathrm{Dom}(A)`. The spectral
measure $`\mu^U` lives on $`\sigma(U)`, inside the unit circle, and gives no mass to the point
$`1`. With $`D(u) = i(u + 1)/(u - 1)` the inverse of the Cayley map,
$`A = \int D \, d\mu^U`, and $`\mu^A(E) = \mu^U(D^{-1}(E))` is a projection-valued measure on
$`\mathbb{R}` with $`A = \int \lambda \, d\mu^A`.

Three choices differ from the blueprint's text; the blueprint records only the second:

* {apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.cayleyPVM}[`cayleyPVM`] composes with $`D` directly. The blueprint restricts $`\mu^U` to the
  circle without $`1` and transports it along the Cayley map; the two constructions it uses,
  {apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.restrictPVM}[`restrictPVM`] and {apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.mapPVM}[`mapPVM`], are formalised but not used.
* For uniqueness, a second measure $`\nu` with $`A = \int \lambda \, d\nu` is carried to the
  circle along the Cayley map and compared there with $`\mu^U`
  ({apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.eq_of_integral_id_eq_ambient}[`eq_of_integral_id_eq_ambient`]). The blueprint transports $`\nu` along the
  bijection onto the circle without $`1`; the library pushes it forward to the whole circle
  ({apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.pushforwardPVM}[`pushforwardPVM`]), which needs no bijectivity (formalization note after the proof
  of [thrm:hall-10.4](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#thrm:hall-10.4)).
* The blueprint assumes $`H \neq \{0\}`. Some intermediate lemmas carry `Nontrivial H`; the
  spectral theorem treats $`H = \{0\}` separately and has no such hypothesis.

The spectral measure lives on $`\mathbb{R}`, as in the blueprint, not on the spectrum as in the
bounded case; that it is concentrated on $`\sigma(A)` is a separate theorem.

