## The spectrum and essential self-adjointness

Blueprint: [def:hall-9.16][ub-916], [lmm:uniqueness-of-resolvent][ub-ures],
[lmm:spectrum-notions-agree][ub-agree], [thrm:hall-9.17][ub-917], [thrm:hall-9.21][ub-921].

{docstring Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.subSmul}

{docstring Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.IsResolvent}

{docstring Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.pmapSpectrum}

The spectrum of a self-adjoint operator is real:

```lean (name := checkReal)
open Physicslib4 Spectral Unbounded in
#check pmapSpectrum_subset_real
```

```leanOutput checkReal
Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.pmapSpectrum_subset_real.{u_1} {H : Type u_1} [NormedAddCommGroup H]
  [InnerProductSpace ℂ H] [CompleteSpace H] {T : H →ₗ.[ℂ] H} (hsa : IsSelfAdjoint T) : pmapSpectrum T ⊆ {z | z.im = 0}
```

The inverse of $`T - \lambda\mathbf{1}` is unique
({apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.existsUnique_isResolvent}[`existsUnique_isResolvent`]),
the two notions of spectrum agree on bounded operators
({apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.pmapSpectrum_toPMap_top}[`pmapSpectrum_toPMap_top`]),
and non-real $`\lambda` are in the resolvent set of a self-adjoint operator
({apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.mem_pmapResolventSet_of_im_ne_zero}[`mem_pmapResolventSet_of_im_ne_zero`]).
The criterion for essential self-adjointness, with `subSmul T Complex.I` the operator
$`T - i\mathbf{1}` and `subSmul T (-Complex.I)` the operator $`T + i\mathbf{1}`:

```lean (name := checkEssSA)
open Physicslib4 Spectral Unbounded in
#check isEssentiallySelfAdjoint_iff_dense_range
```

```leanOutput checkEssSA
Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.isEssentiallySelfAdjoint_iff_dense_range.{u_1} {H : Type u_1} [NormedAddCommGroup H]
  [InnerProductSpace ℂ H] [CompleteSpace H] {T : H →ₗ.[ℂ] H} (hT : HasDenseDomain T) (hsym : IsSymmetric T) :
  IsEssentiallySelfAdjoint T ↔ Dense ↑(range (subSmul T Complex.I)) ∧ Dense ↑(range (subSmul T (-Complex.I)))
```

