## The abstract functional calculus

Blueprint: [def:abstract-continuous-functional-calculus][ub-acfc],
[def:abstract-associated-measures][ub-aam], [prpstn:abstract-extended-forms-are-bounded][ub-aef],
[def:abstract-extended-calculus][ub-aec], [thrm:abstract-calculus-yields-pvm][ub-apvm],
[thrm:hall-10.20][ub-1020].

{docstring Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.IsAbstractCalculus}

{docstring Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.abstractPVM}

The measures $`\mu_\psi` and the extended calculus are
{apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.abstractMeasure}[`abstractMeasure`] and
{apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.extendedCalculus}[`extendedCalculus`]; the latter, like the
bounded integral, is $`0` on functions that are not bounded and measurable. The spectral theorem
for bounded normal operators:

```lean (name := checkNormal)
open Physicslib4 Spectral Unbounded in
#check existsUnique_spectralMeasure_normal
```

```leanOutput checkNormal
Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.existsUnique_spectralMeasure_normal.{u_1} {H : Type u_1} [NormedAddCommGroup H]
  [InnerProductSpace ℂ H] [CompleteSpace H] {A : H →L[ℂ] H} (hA : IsStarNormal A) :
  ∃! μ, (μ.integral fun lam => ↑lam) = A
```

Here `μ` ranges over `ProjectionValuedMeasure (spectrum ℂ A) H`, and the integrand is
$`\lambda` on the complex spectrum. It has the form of the bounded self-adjoint theorem of the
{ref "spectral-main"}[bounded chapter], with `spectrum ℂ A` in place of `spectrum ℝ A`.
The ambient form of uniqueness, for a measure on a larger compact set such as the unit circle, is
{apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Unbounded.eq_of_integral_id_eq_ambient}[`eq_of_integral_id_eq_ambient`].

